# CODEOWNERS — routing and labelling for the files where a wrong line has a
# different consequence from every other file in this repository.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE ENFORCES, AND SINCE WHEN (2026-10-15).
#
# 1. THE CATCH-ALL DECIDES WHO IS ASKED. `.github/rulesets/lead-review.json`
#    turns on "require review from Code Owners" for main, stage and dev, and
#    every path here names `@darshani8`, the repository owner and lead. So every
#    pull request a collaborator opens asks the owner for an approval, and a
#    push after that approval asks again. The same ruleset lets only the
#    repository admin merge at all, so the approval is the second line, not
#    the first. The owner's own pull requests (and the ones Claude opens in the
#    owner's sessions, which are authored by the same account) cannot be
#    approved by their author; the admin merges them through the ruleset's
#    pull-request-only bypass. Do NOT add a collaborator as an owner of any
#    path: their approval would satisfy that second line for everyone else.
#
# 2. IT STILL DOES NOT SEPARATE THE SHARP PATHS IN THE REVIEW UI. One owner
#    holds every file, so a one-character edit to `student_data_egress_allowed`
#    asks the same person as a typo fix in a comment. The paths below are an
#    INVENTORY for the human reading this file, with the consequence written
#    beside each one; if you cannot write that paragraph, the path does not
#    belong here.
#
# A PR that edits this file is itself owned by `@darshani8` (last section), and
# GitHub reads CODEOWNERS from the BASE branch, so a pull request cannot approve
# its own change to who approves.
#
# GitHub semantics that matter when editing this file: the LAST matching pattern
# wins, not the most specific. Keep the catch-all first and the sharp paths last.

